|passage=Some '''alloparenting''' relationships among animals resemble adoption, although more resemble foster care or wet-nursing.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1997|author=C. Sue Carter; R. Lucille Roberts|chapter=9: The Psychobiological Basis of Cooperative Breeding in Rodents|editors=Nancy G. Solomon; Jeffrey A. French|title=Cooperative Breeding in Mammals|publisher={{w|Cambridge University Press}}|year_published=2007|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=6_brrHFGmEcC&pg=PA256&dq=%22alloparenting%22%7C%22alloparentings%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwil8JP2n_nWAhVI1oMKHdizAscQ6AEITjAH#v=onepage&q=%22alloparenting%22%7C%22alloparentings%22&f=false|page=256
|passage=The behaviors associated with '''alloparenting''' are phenotypically similar to maternal and paternal care.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2016|author=Abigail A. Marsh|chapter=9: Extraordinary Altruism: A Cognitive Neuroscience Perspective|editors=Joshua David Greene; India Morrison; Martin E. P. Seligman|title=Positive Neuroscience|publisher={{w|Oxford University Press}}|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=dNCSDAAAQBAJ&pg=PA150&dq=%22alloparenting%22%7C%22alloparentings%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwil8JP2n_nWAhVI1oMKHdizAscQ6AEIfjAP#v=onepage&q=%22alloparenting%22%7C%22alloparentings%22&f=false|page=150
|passage=Characteristics of species in which '''alloparenting''' occurs include organization into small, close-knit, and cooperative groups and infants being born relatively helpless and dependent on care from adults.}}

